<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kamehameha I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1758–1819</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Kamehameha the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kamehameha I was a chief of Hawaiʻi Island who conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molokai">Molokaʻi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a> in a series of campaigns, winning the decisive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nuuanu">Battle of Nuʻuanu</a> in 1795. He founded the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauai">Kauaʻi</a> accepted his rule in 1810. He used Western ships and firearms, controlled the sandalwood trade and kept the traditional kapu system until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i.jpg" alt="Kamehameha I" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis Choris · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_Choris_(1795-1828)_-_Kamehameha,_King_of_the_Sandwich_Islands_by_Louis_Choris,_(Russian),_pen_and_watercolor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
